{"language_code":"eng","language_probability":1.0,"text":"One dollar, doubling every day, outgrows all the personal wealth on Earth. Guess how many days it takes? Day zero. One crisp dollar bill on a kitchen table. Day ten. It's one thousand twenty-four dollars. Fits in a wallet. Day twenty. Just over a million. It needs the whole house. Day thirty. Over a billion. The street is full of money. Day forty. One point one trillion dollars. More than the richest person alive. Day forty-eight. Two hundred eighty-one trillion. Just over half the world's personal wealth, about five hundred seventeen trillion dollars. Then, one more doubling. Day forty-nine. Five hundred sixty-three trillion. The dollar is bigger than everyone. Luckily, no real investment doubles every day. Unluckily, that includes yours. Doubling is a snowball rolling downhill. The last day alone added as much as the forty-eight before it. At the stock market's average of roughly ten percent a year, the same trip takes about three hundred fifty years. So, the answer was forty-nine days, or three hundred fifty years if you want to keep the dollar.","words":[{"text":"One","start":0.1,"end":0.24,"type":"word","logprob":-0.10040337592363358},{"text":" ","start":0.24,"end":0.32,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.000037788631743751466},{"text":"dollar,","start":0.32,"end":0.64,"type":"word","logprob":-0.0010708366129879973},{"text":" ","start":0.64,"end":1.06,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.000012040065485052764},{"text":"doubling","start":1.06,"end":1.4,"type":"word","logprob":-0.000012040065485052764},{"text":" ","start":1.4,"end":1.56,"type":"spacing","logprob":-7.152555099310121e-7},{"text":"every","start":1.56,"end":1.74,"type":"word","logprob":-7.152555099310121e-7},{"text":" ","start":1.74,"end":1.84,"type":"spacing","logprob":0.0},{"text":"day,","start":1.84,"end":2.06,"type":"word","logprob":-0.004038001410663128},{"text":" ","start":2.06,"end":2.58,"type":"spacing","logprob":-0.000030517112463712692},{"text":"